00:05:38.000 --> 00:06:31.000
So I became interested in meditation very early on in my career. I had the opportunity to be around some people who, were meditators and they just impressed me with their demeanor. They were very warm-hearted people, really friendly. They were the kind of people I wanted to be around and they weren't for the most part my professors in graduate school. There were folks I met on the outside and they all had in common this interest in practice of meditation. And that's what led me to explore this in the first place. And I actually did do some research very early on in my career and actually published some of that in the 1970s on meditation, but it was made very clear to me by my professors at that time
您也曾撰文谈到它如何一直是您生活中重要的一部分。但在您职业生涯的前几十年里，它并不是您学术研究的一部分。据我了解，实际上是达赖喇嘛激发了您在该领域的研究所。您能讲讲那个故事吗？当然，我很乐意讲。所以我很早就对冥想产生了兴趣。我有机会接触到一些练习冥想的人，他们的风度给我留下了深刻的印象。他们是非常热心肠的人，真的很友好。他们是我愿意与之交往的那种人，而且他们绝大多数都不是我在研究生院的教授。他们是我在外面遇到的人，他们都有一个共同点，那就是对冥想实践的兴趣。正是这一点促使我首先探索这个领域。事实上，我在职业生涯的早期确实做过一些研究，并且在20世纪70年代发表了一些关于冥想的研究，但当时的教授向我明确表示，

10
00:06:31.000 --> 00:07:23.000
that if I wanted a successful career in science, that was a terrible way to begin. And I better find some other topic. And so I was actually a very dutiful student, and that's when I began to pursue research on the brain and emotion and kept my interest in meditation very much under the radar. And then when I met the Dalai Lama for the first time was in 1992, and he very directly challenged me. And he said, look, you've been using tools of modern neuroscience to study stress and anxiety and depression. Why can't you use those same tools to study kindness and to study compassion? and I didn't have a very good answer for him, but it was a wake-up call, and that was the beginning of a major pivot
如果我想在科学界获得成功的职业生涯，那将是一个糟糕的开端。我最好找点别的课题。所以实际上我是一个非常听话的学生，正是在那个时候，我开始从事大脑和情绪方面的研究，并将我对冥想的兴趣深深地隐藏在幕后。然后我于1992年第一次见到了达赖喇嘛，他非常直接地向我提出了挑战。他说，听着，你一直在用现代神经科学的工具来研究压力、焦虑和抑郁。为什么你不能用同样的工具来研究善良和同情心呢？我当时答不上来，但这对我来说是一个警钟，那也是我们工作中重大转变的开始，

11
00:07:23.000 --> 00:08:19.000
that we made in our work and oriented more toward the positive qualities of our nature and how they can be cultivated. What happened with those professors who were so discouraging? Have you spoken to them since? Some of them. You know, I think some of them came around and some of them, you know, ended their careers in a way that I think is a little bit close-minded. But now, if you look at textbooks of psychology or hard-nosed journals, the best journals in the world, really, they are publishing work in this area. And one of the commitments I made to the Dalai Lama in 1992 is I was going to do everything I could to put compassion squarely within the crosshairs of rigorous science.
我们的工作开始更多地转向我们天性中的积极品质以及如何培养这些品质。那些曾经极力泼冷水的教授们后来怎么样了？您后来跟他们说过话吗？其中一些人。你知道，我认为他们中的一些人后来转变了态度，而另一些人，你知道，他们职业生涯的结局在我看来有点思想闭塞。但现在，如果你看看心理学教科书或严谨的期刊，世界上最好的期刊，真的，他们都在发表这个领域的研究成果。我在1992年对达赖喇嘛做出过承诺之一，就是我要尽我所能，将同情心置于严谨科学的精准审视之下。

